{"name":"Ramus / vertical ramus augmentation","deep":true,"image":null,"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["The ramus is the vertical branch of the lower jaw rising from the jaw angle toward the ear. Augmenting it adds height there, usually with an implant, so the jawline looks longer and more defined from the side.","It is discussed mainly as one part of a custom wrap-around jaw implant design. The published medical literature on ramus implants concerns dental anchorage and jaw reconstruction; no study of ramus augmentation for appearance was found."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People generally approach it as a component of a custom implant plan made with a surgeon, rather than a standalone item. The shape change shows after healing."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Risks have not been studied for the cosmetic use. General implant risks include infection, displacement and nerve injury near the jaw."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[],"links":{"studies":[{"title":"Immediately provisionalized OsseoSpeed(™) Profile implants inserted into extraction sockets: 3-year results.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26300062/","source":"Clinical oral implants research","year":2016,"cites":30,"kind":null},{"title":"Experimental and numerical predictions of Biomet(®) alloplastic implant in a cadaveric mandibular ramus.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27017105/","source":"Journal of cranio-maxillo-facial surgery : official publication of the European Association for Cranio-Maxillo-Facial Surgery","year":2016,"cites":11,"kind":null},{"title":"Tripod Fixation of Periacetabular Metastatic Lesions Using the IlluminOss Device.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39280962/","source":"JBJS essential surgical techniques","year":2024,"cites":1,"kind":null},{"title":"The ramus implant: a report on observations following its use.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/5285110/","source":"Oral implantology","year":1971,"cites":1,"kind":null},{"title